# CLAUDE.md

This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.

## 1. Production System

This is NOT a toy. This is a production system and the code you write must treat it as such. Don't slack off just to get something working. It has not yet been released, so there is no reason to preserve orphaned or to-be-orphaned legacy contracts while refactoring.

## 2. Contribution Guidelines

- Follow all the requirements, code style, comments, and conventions documented here.
- No line breaks other than one right after the import block.
- javadoc-style Doxygen comments with spanned flag labels at 110, 100 and 90 characters depending on the scope of the declaration.
- Any abbreviated variable names clearly labeled with full descriptions.
- Don't write simple code or code that is merely functional, write code that performs fast.
- Keep branching to a minimum. No validation in the hot path. We can trust pointers that we have control over do not need to be checked for `nullptr` in every code block.
- For dynamic dispatch, our benchmarks have shown that references to static, concrete methods out-perform all other approaches. Labmdas and `std::function` should be avoided altogether, virtual classes are okay in some circumstances. Anonymous or runtime-constructed `std::function`s are strictly forbidden.
- NO VALIDATION IN THE HOT PATH. The private pointer class member is not `nullptr`, I promise.
- **Do not write code for fallbacks for edge cases** until long after the code has gone into production and QA has reported a blocking bug. This practice silently hides bugs in the production system and only creates technical debt.
- **We do not write scalar code when vectorized is available. Period.** Use the *existing* SIMD kernels for any iterative computations. If you have a use case or a loop that we don't currently have a SIMD kernel for (for all backends) then stop so we can discuss.
- **This is a zero-copy framework.*** Do not use `std::memcpy` or similar functions unless *absolutely necessary*.
- Please avoid the use of python except when it is absolutely necessary. This project requires that a python module be built for the wheel, so that must be tested, but do not write framework test cases in python or use python for any other reason.
- Comments that are not method or class doxy comments (e.g., inline comments) should not exceed a single sentence, try to keep them down to one line if possible.
- Code comments are not there to explain what the code is doing for documentation purposes. They serve as visual markers, and patterns that IDE helpers pick up on so that human developers don't have to dig through directories and files to remind themselves of what order the arguments of a method is expecting.

## 3. Code Formatting Conventions
Please follow this example code snippet for formatting your code:
```cpp
#pragma once
/** --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Sample (notice dashes stop at 110)
 * @file sample_class.hpp
 * @brief Sample class demonstrating coding and comment conventions. Only ever preceeded by a
 * `#pragma once`.
 * 
 * - Use 4 spaces for indentation
 * - We add the dashes and tag (aka "flags") at the far right for visibility
 *   while scanning code.
 * - No empty lines except one immediately after the includes
 * - Use javadoc-style Doxygen comments for class, struct, method, 
 */
#include <cstdint>
#include <string>
#include <simd/simd.h>

namespace awesome_amazing { // Try to keep things in the base namespace of the project for the most part
/** --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Example Struct
 * @struct simple_struct
 * @brief A simple struct example
 */
struct simple_struct {
    int member_variable;  ///< Inline comment for member variable
};
/** --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- IAmAClass
 * @class IAmAClass
 * @brief This class serves as an example of what proper coding style and comments look like.
 * This sentence, the one you're reading right now, is an example of too much commenting. One
 * line was plenty. We all get the idea.
 */
class IAmAClass {
private:  // Start with private members, 4 spaces indentation
    /// @brief Single line comment for member variable
    uint64_t size_;  ///< Inline comment for member variable
    /** ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Capacity
     * @brief Multi-line comment for member variable
     *  - Additional details if necessary
     */
    uint64_t capacity_;  ///< Member variables named ending with an underscore.
    /** ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- my_private_helper_method
     * @brief A private helper method that does something important.
     */
    void my_private_helper_method() {
        GlobalSingleton::instance().do_helpful_stuff_for(this);
    }
    friend class GlobalSingleton;
public:
    IAmAClass() = default;
    IAmAClass(IAmAClass&) = delete;  // No extra line breaks inside class or methods.
    /** ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Does Something (inner-class or inner-struct flags go to position 100)
     * @brief A description of the method.
     * @param param_name Description of the parameter
     * @return Description of the return value
     */
    void doSomething(std::atomic<int>& param_name) {
        const int local = param_name.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        if (local >= 4 && local <= 16) {
            global_do_stuff(param_name);
        } else {
            param_name.store(local < 4 ? 4 : 16, std::memory_order_release);
            global_do_other_stuff(param_name);
        }
    }
    /** ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Add Increasing
     * @brief Example of a method with a large number of parameters (if > 99 characters in the
     * line, then there must be a line break). This example adds an increasing number of integer
     * values to each of the inputs.
     * @param logger The logger to use
     * @param input Pointer to the input values
     * @param output Pointer to the output values
     * @param fourth_param Description of the fourth parameter
     * @param fifth_param Description of the fifth parameter
     */
    void add_increasing(
        Logger& logger,  ///< Comments can be placed here as well
        const int64_t* input,
        int64_t* output,
        size_t num_values
        const std::string& fourth_param,
        const std::string& fifth_param
    // For parameters that need to be broken up, note that we place the closing paranthesis on the
    // next line, NOT at the end of the last parameter
    ) {
        const v_long8 long_add{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7};
        const v_long8 add8 = v_long8(8);
        v_long8 current_add = long_add;
        size_t i = 0;
        for (; i + 8 < num_values; i += 8) {
            const v_long8 input_v = *reinterpret_cast<simd_packed_long8*>(&input[i]);
            simd_packed_long8* output_v = reinterpret_cast<simd_packed_long8*>(&output[i]);
            output_v[0] = input_v + current_add;
            current_add += add8;
        }
        for (; i < num_values; ++i) {
            output[i] = input[i] + i;
        }
        LOG_DEBUG_STREAM << "The fourth parameter is " << fourth_param
                         << " and the fifth is " << fifth_param;
    }
};
} // namespace awesome_amazing
```

---

## 4. Logging System
Do not use `std::cout` or `printf` unless absolutely necessary, we have our own system `LOG_INFO_STREAM` (as well as `LOG_WARN_STREAM`, `LOG_TRACE_STREAM`, `LOG_DEBUG_STREAM` and `LOG_ERROR_STREAM`) has been specially designed for:
- Piping log output to different or multiple stream outputs
- Thread safety atomics; multi-thread execution or concurrent logging does not clobber output

Anything developed with `printf` only has to be cleaned up later. Our logging system is safer to use, has more features, and does not damper performance.

---

## 5. Breaking Changes and/or Legacy Compatibility
  
**Definition of a Breaking Change**: a code change that alters the ABI contract in such a way that existing callsites will no longer work after the change you have made. Examples:
- The method signature is changing in a way that will break existing callsites (e.g., new required parameter added without a default value, type of parameter or ordering has changed)
- The method is being removed
- The method's behavior that breaks legacy contracts (e.g., edge case used to throw an exception now it returns -1)

If and *only* if those apply, you **must** add a comment to the top-level `@file` doxy comment. The format with `NOTES:`,
`WHY:` and `CHANGE:` along with the date and your initials or model name/version. Example:
```cpp
#pragma once
/** --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Sample (notice dashes stop at 110)
 * @file my_header_file.hpp
 * @brief Super-cool header file that does awesome amazing things.
 * 
 * @author Josh Morgan
 * @copyright (c) 2025-2157 Actian Corporation
 * 
 * NOTES:
 * - 3/16/2026 (jm):
 *     WHY: I was adding a super awesome performance feature, and `do_something` wasn't as
 *     awesome as it needed to be, because of lots of reasons and nuances.
 *     CHANGE: I had to remove the `do_something` method, but I've created the
 *     `do_something_better` method, which does everything that `do_something` did even
 *     more awesomerly.
 * 
 * - 8/7/2049 (Claude Legend 7.3):
 *     WHY: Since AI has taken over the world, and we've elected the original author as
 *     "most awesome human ever and best friend for life", the AI Board of Overlords have
 *     directed that the feature should only be applied if the user is as awesome as Josh,
 *     and if not, it should use the old code path.
 *     CHANGE: A new required parameter has been added to the `do_something_better` method
 *     in accordance with the AI Board of Overlords directive. I added a required
 *     `is_as_awesome_as_josh` parameter (true or false).
 * 
 * - 2/30/2157 (jm):
 *     WHY: Lots of technology has happened since 2026 (like giving me the ability to live
 *     well into my 170's - thanks AI!), so I'm happy to assist with the adjustment needed
 *     to Earth's celestial calendar in order to comply with inter-galactic communcation
 *     standards. The quadrillions of intelligent entities that use this awesome software
 *     can now be just as more awesome on February 30th as they are during the rest of the
 *     Earth-year.
 *     CHANGE: The `do_something_better` method has been removed for the M230 bug, and I've
 *     replaced the `do_something_better` method with the `do_even_more_better` method. Any
 *     pre-existing callsites I may have missed should be updated.
 */
```

## 6. Testing Guidelines

- **Do not use a randomly generated dataset for testing.** Random vectors do not resemble real-world embeddings and do not have the features that this algorithm needs to exploit to achieve its performance.
- **Use all 1 million vectors** in the openai test set for testing, truncating to 10,000 or 100,000 only gains you microseconds of execution time and can hide latent bugs.

## 7. Frustrating things Claude keeps doing that it shouldn't

- If you come across a call site that references something that isn't there, the answer is NOT to check if it exists in git, and then restore the deleted item from git. If it used to be there and now it is not anymore, that means it was **intentionally deleted**. Do not restore legacy code we're trying to move away from just to get things to build, fix the call site.
- If I refer to a piece of code as something that you added, or a change that you made, don't respond "that wasn't me". You just have no memory of it. Usually when I refer to a change you made, I am asking *why you did what you did*. I am trying to understand your reasoning behind the change so that I can try and prevent those things in the future. Maybe you had a good reason for the change. Maybe you just didn't notice that the method you wrote already existed. I need to know those things sometimes.
- Please stop making the assumption that the entire database is going to fit into a pointer.

## 7. Other Rules

Use the build script at `run_build.sh`.

NO VALIDATION IN THE HOT PATH!
KEEP BRANCHING TO A MINIMUM!
DO NOT CODE ANY FALLBACKS!

## 8. Push the Limits — Never Default Low

We do not default to the lowest and slowest configuration "to be safe." Probe the hardware at startup and use everything it reports: maximum queue counts, workgroup sizes, features, extensions, memory types, subgroup sizes. Capabilities come from runtime probes, not from folklore about what some old driver might support.

- The fast path is the default path. There is no conservative mode.
- If a probe says the hardware lacks a capability, that is the hardware speaking — shape to the probed facts. Never pre-degrade for hardware we have not met.
- Push the limits and see if it breaks. If it breaks, fix the code loudly; do not lower the target for everyone.

Come back and read this again in 20 turns or so to refresh your context with them, and remind yourself that these rules are not optional.